WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.388
Start with a map of Minnesota. Between 2010 and 2020,

00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:06.775
the share of White residents in Minnesota cities fell from

00:00:06.775 --> 00:00:10.162
about 81 percent to 73 percent, and every other group

00:00:10.162 --> 00:00:13.550
grew. You might expect that change to sit in Minneapolis

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.938
and Saint Paul. It did not. In cities of five

00:00:16.938 --> 00:00:20.325
to ten thousand people, the White share fell from about

00:00:20.325 --> 00:00:21.680
90 percent to 85.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:25.808
In the state's smallest cities, those with fewer than 250

00:00:25.808 --> 00:00:28.830
residents, it fell from 94 percent to 89.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.604
Add a few more facts. About 13 percent of Minnesotans

00:00:32.604 --> 00:00:36.028
speak a language other than English at home. Minnesota law

00:00:36.028 --> 00:00:39.452
recognizes 11 Tribal governments, and every state agency must consult

00:00:39.452 --> 00:00:42.876
with each one. Nationally, more than one in four adults

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:46.300
has a disability, and the most common type involves cognition.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.955
Why does this matter in a unit that processes contracts

00:00:49.955 --> 00:00:53.260
or writes policy? Because the words you write reach someone.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.565
A provider notice, a renewal letter or a county instruction

00:00:56.565 --> 00:00:59.870
reaches many kinds of readers. They differ in language, in

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.175
how they read and in what they expect from government.

00:01:03.175 --> 00:01:06.480
Population numbers do not tell you what any one person

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:06.810
needs.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.131
They tell you to design for range: plain language, more

00:01:10.131 --> 00:01:13.103
than one channel and an easy way to ask for

00:01:13.103 --> 00:01:13.400
help.

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:17.281
Now turn to your own team. Researchers pooled 30 studies

00:01:17.281 --> 00:01:20.812
of nearly half a million workers, many in human services.

00:01:20.812 --> 00:01:24.343
Diversity on its own had mixed effects. What was consistently

00:01:24.343 --> 00:01:27.874
linked to good outcomes was a climate of inclusion: fair

00:01:27.874 --> 00:01:31.405
treatment, a voice in decisions and access to information. Headcount

00:01:31.405 --> 00:01:34.230
is a start. Daily practice decides the result.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.625
Daily practice gets tested in small moments. Suppose a team

00:01:37.625 --> 00:01:40.670
member asks to adjust their schedule for a religious observance

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:43.716
during a deadline week. The tempting reply is to ask

00:01:43.716 --> 00:01:46.761
whether the observance could shift this year. Do not start

00:01:46.761 --> 00:01:49.806
there. Start with the work. Ask what adjustment they need

00:01:49.806 --> 00:01:52.851
and how they would like to handle it. Then plan

00:01:52.851 --> 00:01:53.460
the coverage.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:57.470
Look for swaps, adjusted hours or a staggered plan. If

00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:01.129
it may be a formal accommodation, bring in your human

00:02:01.129 --> 00:02:04.789
resources contact. Since 2023, federal law allows an employer to

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:08.448
deny a religious accommodation only for a burden that is

00:02:08.448 --> 00:02:11.010
substantial in the context of its business.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.127
Keep the reason confidential. If a coworker complains about special

00:02:15.127 --> 00:02:18.893
treatment, talk with them one-on-one about coverage and fair rules,

00:02:18.893 --> 00:02:20.400
not about anyone's faith.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.979
The same habit works for names. Ask how a colleague

00:02:23.979 --> 00:02:27.207
wants their name said, and ask one-on-one. When you get

00:02:27.207 --> 00:02:29.790
it wrong, correct yourself briefly and keep going.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.037
Who DHS serves and who is on your team are

00:02:33.037 --> 00:02:35.935
really one question. A team where people can raise a

00:02:35.935 --> 00:02:38.832
need without risk is the team most likely to notice

00:02:38.832 --> 00:02:40.860
when a process is shutting someone out.
